\title{AgriFusionR: an integration framework for agricultural analytics}
\description{
R is already well supplied with machine learning, geospatial and
agronomic packages. What it lacks is the layer that joins them: a single
object that holds a management unit and its season, keeps climate, soil and
remote-sensing covariates aligned to it, aggregates them over the crop's
phenology rather than the calendar, and validates the result in a way that
survives spatial autocorrelation. That layer is what this package provides.
}
\section{Getting started}{

\code{\link[=agri_project]{agri_project()}} builds the object; \code{\link[=add_climate]{add_climate()}} and its siblings attach
covariates; \code{\link[=phenology_windows]{phenology_windows()}} derives thermal-time stages;
\code{\link[=build_features]{build_features()}} reduces everything to one row per unit and season;
\code{\link[=check_project]{check_project()}} looks for leakage and other faults; \code{\link[=train_model]{train_model()}} fits
and validates; \code{\link[=explain]{explain()}}, \code{\link[=uncertainty]{uncertainty()}} and \code{\link[=report]{report()}} interpret.
}

\section{Extending it}{

\code{\link[=register_source]{register_source()}} and \code{\link[=register_learner]{register_learner()}} add data providers and
algorithms without modifying the package.
}

